<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vo Nguyen Giap</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/2013">2013</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as General Vo Nguyen Giap</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vo Nguyen Giap was a Vietnamese military commander, the principal general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> and later of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. He led the Viet Minh forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> in 1954 forced the surrender of the French garrison, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vo-nguyen-giap.jpg" alt="Vo Nguyen Giap" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mr._Vo_Nguyen_Giap_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Võ Nguyên Giáp was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quang-binh-province">Quảng Bình province</a>, Annam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>, into a relatively comfortable peasant family. His father, a minor official and committed nationalist who had taken part in the Cần Vương movement in the 1880s, was arrested by the French colonial authorities in 1919 and died in prison a few weeks later. In 1924, at the age of thirteen, Giáp left home to attend the Quốc Học, a French-run lycée in Huế. He was expelled after two years for taking part in protests, and joined the underground Tân Việt Revolutionary Party, which introduced him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>.</p><p>He was arrested in 1930 for taking part in student protests and served 13 months of a two-year sentence in Lao Bảo Prison. He studied law, worked as a history teacher in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> and wrote for revolutionary newspapers. He had no military training before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 3 May 1940, after the French outlawed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a>, Giáp went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a>. His wife, left behind, was arrested and sentenced to fifteen years, and in 1943 he was told that guards in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>'s central prison had beaten her to death. In 1942 he and about forty men moved back into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and settled in caves near Vu Nhai, forming the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>. On 25 December 1944 he led successful attacks on the French outposts at Khai Phat and Na Ngan.</p><p>He served as military commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> and the People's Army from 1941 to 1972, as minister of defence in 1946 to 1947 and from 1948 to 1980, and as deputy prime minister from 1955 to 1991. He turned a disorganised band of rebels into a light-infantry army with artillery and advanced logistics, and won the decisive victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> in 1954. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> he commanded the army during the Tet Offensive of 1968 and the Easter Offensive of 1972, and he was the principal architect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> trail through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. He oversaw the campaigns in Cambodia in 1978 and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Giáp resigned as defence minister in 1980 and left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> in 1982. He wrote extensively on military theory, including People's Army, People's War and We Will Win. He died on 4 October 2013 at Central Military Hospital 108 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, aged 102, and was given a state funeral on 12 and 13 October, before burial in his home province of Quảng Bình. Several Vietnamese cities renamed prominent streets after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Võ Nguyên Giáp” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The catastrophic defeat that broke the second empire came at Dien Bien Phu in May 1954, where <b>General Vo Nguyen Giap</b>'s Viet Minh besieged a French garrison and forced its surrender after fifty-six days.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lộc Thủy, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.24,106.75&amp;year=1977" aria-label="Lộc Thủy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7452 1524 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vo Nguyen Giap"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7902 1794h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7902 1794h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.24,106.75&amp;year=1977">What was here in 1977? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
